[background arg="res://assets/temp/train_interior_day.jpg" fade="0.0"]
audio -
A sharp jolt from the train tracks snapped me awake.
Through the window, I see it—Nillayah City. A sprawling metropolis covering the entire island, with massive ports below handling shipments from across the world.
Two days and three nights of packing and commuting. That’s what it took to finally get here.
join yunira (neutral) left
yunira: To think this tiny island was just a fishing village a generation ago.
Through the glass pane, I see large shipping containers being craned over from docked ships.
The containers are labeled with familiar logos, recognizable no matter where you’re from.
Yunira, my older sister, scrolls through her phone absently. Her eyes drift to my luggage tucked under the seat.
join yunira (concerned) left
yunira (concerned): Hey. Sit down properly. Someone might steal your stuff.
- "Whatever, Gramps."
	Yunira grabs Player’s ear and yanks them away from window
	audio -
	yunira (annoyed): Excuse me?!
	yunira (annoyed): We’re literally two years apart. You should be thanking me for babysitting you.
	player: Babysitting?! Yunira, I’m literally enrolling for the academy. THE ACADEMY, mind you. Do you know how hard it is to even get accepted?
	yunira (sigh): Sighs... I know, but I also know they don’t enroll young brats without manners. Sit down.
	player: ...Ugh, you sound like Granny. That’s why you’re "The Gramps," trademarked with a little ‘c’ on it.
	yunira (concerned): ...What does that even—
	yunira (sigh): Okay, you know what, whatever. You and your nonsensical words... How did Bintang Utama Academy even let this nutcase in?
	player: Because I aced the Standard Proficiency in Magis (SPM) test, remember?
	player: Those Mana Constructs were no match for my strong mana fundamentals!
	audio -
	yunira (giggle): Yeah, and that explains your hard and thick brain.
	player: Very funny, but at least I won’t work in a cubicle like you, Mrs. Nine-Till-Five.
	Yunira ruffles Player’s hair and pinches their cheek
	yunira (annoyed): I swear you’re so annoying!
	player: Ow—Hey! Cut it out, I’m not a kid anymore.
- "You're right. Sorry."
	yunira (neutral): I’m glad you’re taking it seriously. This city has a way of swallowing people who aren't paying attention. Being a caster doesn’t make you an exception, rather a target no less.
	player: I just want to make sure I’m ready for the Academy.
- [Sit down without responding]
yunira (serious): I expect you to behave once the semester starts. I know how talented you were to ace that exam. But please…
yunira (concerned): Don’t mess this up.
The mood quickly became heavy for a moment.
- [Nod Reluctantly]
	I gave her a reluctant nod. It seemed to cheer her up.
	A  gentle smile was painted on her face.
yunira (gentle): You’ll be the first Magis Caster of the family.
yunira (gentle): That fact alone changes lives. I’m certain you will meet many people with all kinds of intentions. Some become casters for fame or prestige—not for knowledge.
yunira (happy): So never forget that you started with curiosity in your passion. Never let others make you think otherwise.
leave yunira